#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116185} :activity/id #uuid "7611282f-defa-5f44-9b26-7ae740be2330" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049247 :topic/id #uuid "e6870227-56f3-5cd6-935a-bdbc42221057" :topic/math-academy-id 1707} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116185 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116516 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the function\n\n$$\ny=5\\log_6(x-2).\n$$\n\nHow do we plot its graph?\n\nAs usual, we can do this by combining transformations of the curve $y=\\log_6 x.$\n\n- We start with the graph of $y=\\log_6 x{:}$\n\n![](images/a8/a85eb96e169e43568da76e33ee7f997af9196d26e867d7e7db1b2ddea0725d2a.png)\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=\\log_6{(x-2)}.$\n\n![](images/24/248a60c13ac422aeace5ba19f9f25716b188c19dbe04f5970287c587adb44c85.png)\n\nNotice that points $(1,0)$ and $(6,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 x$ map to the points $(3,0)$ and $(8,1)$ on the graph of $y=\\log_6 (x-2),$ respectively.\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=5\\log_6{(x-2)}.$\n\n![](images/a6/a67b409068f67d81f0217aaeb30f76bd63c6501376d2adc567db7c4f319d7144.png)\n\nSo, to plot the curve of a transformed logarithmic function, we apply subsequent transformations presented in the given function, as usual." :tutorial/id #uuid "005acbf3-bad3-536d-97e6-c62f95b655d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "46fc00a5-e0fa-5a99-888c-5785294d6366" :step/math-academy-id 20911 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116186}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116186 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115105 :question/id #uuid "4228cae9-1914-59e9-8ed6-48b3edf92638" :question/math-academy-id "e-5734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9e91fd-9896-5fdd-8a60-b8ec86ca761c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 175921